</style></head><body><div class="wrap">
<header><span class="eb">Console</span><h1>GhostSpiral</h1>
<span class="hint">127.0.0.1 · argv composed server-side · funds need arming</span></header>
<div class="cols">
<div>
  <!-- progress rail: one row, current step highlighted -->
  <ol class="stepbar" id="stepbar">
    <li data-s="1"><span class="sn">1</span>Action</li>
    <li data-s="2"><span class="sn">2</span><span id="lbl2">Details</span></li>
    <li data-s="3"><span class="sn">3</span>Privacy</li>
    <li data-s="4"><span class="sn">4</span>Network</li>
    <li data-s="5"><span class="sn">5</span>Run</li>
  </ol>

  <!-- STEP 1 — what are you doing -->
  <section class="step" data-step="1">
    <h2>Step 1 · What do you want to do?</h2>
    <div class="choices" id="mode">
      <button class="choice" data-m="send" aria-pressed="true">
        <span class="ct">Send · BTC → XMR</span>
        <span class="cd">Swap your BTC to Monero through ThorChain, then mix it across fresh subaddresses.</span></button>
      <button class="choice" data-m="receive" aria-pressed="false">
        <span class="ct">Receive · XMR in</span>
        <span class="cd">Make a fresh receive address for a sender, then mix the Monero once it arrives.</span></button>
    </div>
    <div class="stepnav"><span></span><button class="nx" data-go="2">Next →</button></div>
  </section>

  <!-- STEP 2 — details (send) / receive -->
  <section class="step" data-step="2" hidden>
    <h2 id="h2-2">Step 2 · Payment</h2>
    <div id="send-fields">
      <div class="f"><label for="btc_entry">BTC entry</label><input id="btc_entry" placeholder="bc1q…"></div>
      <div class="f"><label for="btc_amount">BTC amount</label><input id="btc_amount" placeholder="0.05"></div>
      <div class="f"><label for="split">Split into</label><input id="split" type="number" min="1" max="8" value="1"></div>
      <label class="chk"><input type="checkbox" id="joinmarket">
        <span>JoinMarket tumble first<span class="d">extra BTC-side mixing before the swap</span></span></label>
    </div>
    <div id="recv-fields" hidden>
      <p class="lead"><b>The sender pays Bitcoin. You receive Monero.</b> Three steps, in order —
      each one needs the file the step before it wrote.</p>

      <div class="rstep"><span class="rn">1</span><div>
        <b>Make the Monero destination.</b> A fresh subaddress on your wallet, where the
        swapped XMR will land. <b>This is not the address you give the sender</b> — it never
        leaves your machine.
        <div class="stepacts" data-acts="make_receive"></div>
        <div class="f" style="margin-top:10px"><label for="receive_wallet">Wallet JSON <span class="d">from step 1</span></label>
          <input id="receive_wallet" placeholder="wallet_ab12cd34.json"></div>
      </div></div>

      <div class="rstep"><span class="rn">2</span><div>
        <b>Get the Bitcoin deposit address.</b> This quotes a BTC→XMR swap and prints the BTC
        address <i>and memo</i> to hand the sender. The destination is read from the wallet JSON
        above, so the 95-character Monero address is never retyped — the one field where a typo
        is irreversible. The memo is checked to actually name your address before any
        instructions are printed.
        <div class="f" style="margin-top:10px"><label for="swap_btc">BTC amount the sender will pay</label>
          <input id="swap_btc" placeholder="0.05"></div>
        <div class="f"><label for="pairs_file">Save quote to</label>
          <input id="pairs_file" placeholder="thor_pairs.json"></div>
        <div class="stepacts" data-acts="swap_quote"></div>
        <p class="lead" style="margin-top:8px">The swap provider necessarily learns
        "this BTC deposit → this XMR address". Tor hides who asked, not the link itself —
        that is exactly why the mix afterwards is not optional.</p>
      </div></div>

      <div class="rstep"><span class="rn">3</span><div>
        <b>Wait for the payment.</b> Polls <b>your own wallet</b> only — no block explorer is
        ever asked about that Bitcoin address, so no new party learns it is being watched.
        Reads only, spends nothing; when the money lands it asks what you want to do with it.
        With a quote file it waits for the quoted amount, otherwise for any balance at all.
        <div class="stepacts" data-acts="watch_receive"></div>
        <div class="f" style="margin-top:10px"><label for="expect_total_xmr">Total XMR expected
          <span class="d">all swaps to this address</span></label>
          <input id="expect_total_xmr" placeholder="4.0"></div>
        <div class="f"><label for="split_recv">Number of swaps
          <span class="d">paying that total</span></label>
          <input id="split_recv" type="number" min="1" max="8" value="1"></div>
        <p class="lead" style="margin-top:6px"><b>Fill these in.</b> Without a total the mix does
        <i>not</i> wait: it plans against whatever has landed the moment it starts. If the
        sender pays in several swaps, the ones that arrive later are never mixed — they sit on
        the receive address, which the swap memo names in public. The count tightens the gate
        so one missing swap cannot hide inside the tolerance.</p>
      </div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="stepnav"><button class="bk" data-go="1">← Back</button><button class="nx" data-go="3">Next →</button></div>
  </section>

  <!-- STEP 3 — privacy -->
  <section class="step" data-step="3" hidden>
    <h2>Step 3 · Privacy level</h2>
    <p class="lead">Pick a preset — it sets everything below. Higher privacy means more transactions,
    higher fees and more waiting.</p>
    <div class="presets" id="presets">
      <button data-p="paranoid" aria-pressed="false"><span class="pn">Maximum safe</span><span class="pd">peel + DAG · two layers · slowest</span></button>
      <button data-p="peel" aria-pressed="false"><span class="pn">Peeling chain</span><span class="pd">peel only · 1 dest per tx</span></button>
      <button data-p="churn" aria-pressed="false"><span class="pn">Deep churn</span><span class="pd">20 wallets · depth 6 · DAG</span></button>
      <button data-p="max" aria-pressed="false"><span class="pn">High privacy</span><span class="pd">25 wallets · depth 3 · DAG</span></button>
      <button data-p="balanced" aria-pressed="true"><span class="pn">Balanced</span><span class="pd">12 wallets · depth 2 · DAG</span></button>
      <button data-p="lowfee" aria-pressed="false"><span class="pn">Low fee</span><span class="pd">8 wallets · depth 2 · DAG · p1</span></button>
      <button data-p="fast" aria-pressed="false"><span class="pn">Fast</span><span class="pd">3 wallets · depth 1 · no DAG</span></button>
    </div>
    <div id="preset-note" class="warnbox"></div>
    <details class="adv"><summary>Fine-tune (advanced)</summary>
      <div class="row2" style="margin-top:10px">
        <div class="f" style="grid-template-columns:76px 1fr"><label for="wallets">Wallets</label><input id="wallets" type="number" min="3" max="60" value="10"></div>
        <div class="f" style="grid-template-columns:56px 1fr"><label for="deep">Depth</label><input id="deep" type="number" min="1" max="6" value="2"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="f" style="grid-template-columns:110px 1fr;margin-top:8px">
        <label for="hop_delay">Hop delay</label>
        <select id="hop_delay">
          <option value="">default · 3-12 min per transaction — WEAKEST</option>
          <option value="600-1800">10 - 30 min per transaction</option>
          <option value="900-3600">15 - 60 min per transaction</option>
          <option value="1800-7200">30 min - 2 h per transaction</option>
          <option value="7200-21600">2 - 6 h per transaction</option>
          <option value="21600-86400">6 - 24 h per transaction (best ring-age)</option>
          <option value="86400-259200">1 - 3 days per transaction</option>
        </select>
      </div>
      <div class="d" id="eta" style="margin:-2px 0 8px 0"></div>
      <div class="d" style="margin:-2px 0 8px 0">
        Seconds to wait before broadcasting each transaction. A hop cannot be
        built until the previous output confirms and unlocks (~10 blocks); this
        is added on top. At the default each output is spent at roughly 11-16
        blocks of age — close to the youngest an output can legally be spent,
        while Monero picks ring decoys from a distribution whose bulk sits far
        above that, so the real output tends to be the newest member of its own
        ring. Longer costs time and buys ring-age plausibility.
        <b>PER TRANSACTION, and a run is dozens of them</b> — a peel chain
        relays one per destination, then one hop, one change sweep and one
        withdrawal each, so the wall clock is that count times this delay.
        These options used to be labelled with a run length ("days") that was
        out by an order of magnitude; the line above is computed from the
        settings actually selected, by the same function the pipeline prints
        before it starts.
      </div>
      <label class="chk"><input type="checkbox" id="dag_mixing" checked>
        <span>DAG hop-mixing<span class="d">second round of per-subaddress hops; waits for on-chain confirmation (~20 min longer)</span></span></label>
      <label class="chk"><input type="checkbox" id="peel">
        <span>Peeling chain<span class="d">distribute ONE tx per destination (each spends the last one's change) instead of one fan-out — outputs are never co-created. MUCH slower: ~20 min per destination.</span></span></label>
      <label class="chk"><input type="checkbox" id="allow_clearnet_relay">
        <span style="color:var(--err)">Allow clearnet relay<span class="d">proceed even when monerod would broadcast to raw-IP peers — reveals your IP</span></span></label>
    </details>
    <div class="subsec">
      <div class="subhead"><span>Fee priority</span><span class="mut-s">live from your daemon</span></div>
      <div class="fees" id="fees"></div>
      <div class="fsrc" id="fsrc">loading…</div>
    </div>
    <div class="subsec">
      <div class="subhead"><span>Exit — where the money goes afterwards</span>
        <span class="mut-s">optional</span></div>
      <p class="lead">One Monero address per line. The mix ends with your funds spread
      across many accounts on this wallet; the exit spends them out,
      <b>one transaction per output</b>, never a single collecting sweep — the
      inputs of a transaction are public, so spending them together would prove
      they share an owner and undo the run.</p>
      <div class="f" style="grid-template-columns:1fr">
        <textarea id="exit_to" rows="3" spellcheck="false"
          placeholder="4… or 8… (one per line; leave empty to withdraw nothing)"></textarea>
      </div>
      <p class="lead"><b>Leave it empty and nothing is withdrawn</b> — the funds
      stay on this wallet and the run tells you so. Give <i>several</i> addresses
      and the withdrawal is spread across them: sending everything to one address
      re-joins off-chain exactly what the run separated on-chain, and no amount
      of mixing undoes that.</p>
      <p class="lead mut-s">Format is checked here; the full <b>checksum</b> is
      verified by the pipeline before any funds move — a mistyped Monero address
      is often still well-formed, and a send has no confirmation step and no
      reversal.</p>
      <div id="exitnote" class="warnbox" hidden></div>
    </div>
    <div class="stepnav"><button class="bk" data-go="2">← Back</button><button class="nx" data-go="4">Next →</button></div>
  </section>

  <!-- STEP 4 — network + preflight -->
  <section class="step" data-step="4" hidden>
    <h2>Step 4 · Network &amp; preflight</h2>
    <div class="f"><label for="tor_proxy">Tor proxy</label><input id="tor_proxy" value="socks5h://127.0.0.1:9050"></div>
    <div class="f"><label for="rpc_primary">Wallet RPC</label><input id="rpc_primary" value="http://127.0.0.1:18083"></div>
    <div class="f"><label for="rpc_daemon">Daemon RPC</label><input id="rpc_daemon" value="http://127.0.0.1:18081"></div>
    <div class="f"><label for="wallet_file">Wallet file</label><input id="wallet_file" value="offline.wallet"></div>
    <div id="pwnote" class="warnbox"></div>
    <div id="pfstatus" class="pfbox"></div>
    <div class="pfbtns">
      <button id="pfdetect" class="pfre" type="button">Detect Tor port</button>
      <button id="pfstart" class="pfre" type="button">Start Tor</button>
      <button id="pfre" class="pfre" type="button">re-run preflight now</button>
    </div>
    <p class="lead" style="margin-top:14px">Run the manual checks for detail (they change nothing):</p>
    <div class="stepacts" data-acts="preflight_tor,preflight_egress,preflight_wallet,paranoia_dry"></div>
    <div class="stepnav"><button class="bk" data-go="3">← Back</button><button class="nx" data-go="5">Next →</button></div>
  </section>

  <!-- STEP 5 — review + run -->
  <section class="step" data-step="5" hidden>
    <h2>Step 5 · Review &amp; run</h2>
    <div class="subhead"><span>Command that will run</span><span class="mut-s">composed on the server</span></div>
    <pre class="prev" id="prev">…</pre>
    <ul class="probs" id="probs"></ul>
    <!-- NON-BLOCKING advisories: things that are legal and probably not what
         the operator meant. `probs` stops the run, so it could never carry
         "this receive run has no arrival target and will not wait" -- and an
         empty problem list reads as "everything is set". See run_notes. -->
    <ul class="notes" id="notes"></ul>
    <div class="arm" id="armbox" style="display:none">
      <p><strong>This moves funds.</strong> It signs and broadcasts real transactions. The OPSEC
      preflight (step 4) must pass — it is re-verified on the server when you run, so it cannot be
      skipped. Type <strong id="armword"></strong> to arm.</p>
      <input id="arm" placeholder="type to arm" autocomplete="off">
    </div>
    <div class="stepacts" data-acts="run_pipeline" style="margin-top:12px"></div>
    <details class="adv"><summary>Developer tools (tests &amp; wipe preview)</summary>
      <div class="stepacts" data-acts="units,integration,ipleak,leakaudit,compile" style="margin-top:10px"></div>
    </details>
    <div class="stepnav"><button class="bk" data-go="4">← Back</button><span></span></div>
  </section>
</div>

<div class="rightcol">
  <div class="panel">
    <div class="pbar"><span class="pt" id="pt">Nothing running</span>
      <span class="pill" id="ppill" style="display:none"></span><span class="pm" id="pm"></span>
      <span class="pacts"><button id="stop" class="stop">Stop</button><button id="copy">Copy</button></span></div>
    <div id="out"><div class="idle">Follow the steps on the left. Output from any action shows here.</div></div>
  </div>
</div>
</div></div><script>
const A=__ACTIONS__, ARM=__ARM__, PWSET=__PWSET__, TOK=__TOKEN__;
const HDR={'Content-Type':'application/json','X-GS-Token':TOK};
const $=s=>document.querySelector(s), $$=s=>[...document.querySelectorAll(s)];
let cur=null,timer=null,mode='send',preset='balanced',armed=false;

// EVERY PRESET NAMES ITS OWN hop_delay.
//
// applyPreset set wallets, depth, DAG, peel, priority and clearnet, and never
// touched the delay -- so the preset labelled "MAXIMUM SAFETY — the two
// strongest layers COMBINED" shipped the WEAKEST value of the one setting
// GhostSpiral's own --hop-delay help calls "AN OPSEC PARAMETER", whose default
// it says "is not a strong one": at 3-12 minutes each carrier output is spent
// at roughly 11-16 blocks of age, close to the youngest an output can legally
// be spent, while Monero draws ring decoys from a distribution whose bulk sits
// far above that -- so the real output tends to be the newest member of its
// own ring.
//
// The console had already made the setting REACHABLE (it was not even in
// SCHEMA before, so no dashboard run could set one); this is the other half,
// because a preset that does not set it is the default for everyone who does
// not know the parameter exists.
//
// The times below are from run_eta at each preset's own settings with an exit
// destination, and they are why these are 30 min - 2 h rather than the
// dropdown's strongest option: at 6-24 h the Maximum preset is 38 DAYS.
const PRESETS={
 paranoid:{wallets:10,deep:2,dag_mixing:true,peel:true,fee_priority:3,clearnet:false,
      hop_delay:'1800-7200',
      note:'MAXIMUM SAFETY — the two strongest layers COMBINED. First a PEELING CHAIN: ~10 destinations plus a few decoys are each sent as their OWN transaction, spending the previous change, so nothing is ever co-created in a fan-out an analyst could cluster. THEN a DAG hop round mixes those outputs a second time. Clearnet relay forced OFF, highest priority, and a 30 min - 2 h delay before each broadcast so outputs are not spent at the youngest age a ring can contain. By far the SLOWEST — about 3 days end to end. Use for high-value sends where time is no object.'},
 peel:{wallets:8,deep:1,dag_mixing:false,peel:true,fee_priority:2,clearnet:false,
      hop_delay:'1800-7200',
      note:'PEELING CHAIN ONLY (no second DAG round). ~8 destinations plus decoys are sent ONE TRANSACTION AT A TIME, each spending the previous change — no co-created fan-out. Strong graph unlinkability and fewer transactions than Maximum safe (one round, not two), at the same 30 min - 2 h broadcast delay. About 2 days end to end.'},
 churn:{wallets:20,deep:6,dag_mixing:true,fee_priority:2,clearnet:false,
      hop_delay:'900-3600',
      note:'DEEP CHURN. 20 subaddresses at maximum depth 6 — the longest hop chains, so the funds cross the most intermediate outputs before they settle. Emphasises trail length over breadth. 15 - 60 min before each broadcast; about a day and a half end to end.'},
 max:{wallets:25,deep:3,dag_mixing:true,fee_priority:3,clearnet:false,
      hop_delay:'900-3600',
      note:'25 subaddresses, depth 3, DAG hops on, priority 3, clearnet relay off. Wide anonymity set. 15 - 60 min before each broadcast; about a day and a half end to end.'},
 balanced:{wallets:12,deep:2,dag_mixing:true,fee_priority:2,clearnet:false,
      hop_delay:'600-1800',
      note:'12 subaddresses, depth 2, DAG hops on, priority 2, clearnet relay off. Sensible default for most amounts. 10 - 30 min before each broadcast; about half a day end to end.'},
 lowfee:{wallets:8,deep:2,dag_mixing:true,fee_priority:1,clearnet:false,
      hop_delay:'600-1800',
      note:'8 subaddresses, depth 2, DAG hops on, priority 1 (cheapest fees). Real mixing on a budget — fewer hops means lower total fees, worth it for smaller amounts where fees would otherwise eat the balance. 10 - 30 min before each broadcast; under half a day end to end.'},
 fast:{wallets:3,deep:1,dag_mixing:false,fee_priority:1,clearnet:false,
      hop_delay:'',
      note:'3 subaddresses, depth 1, no DAG round, priority 1, and the DEFAULT 3-12 min broadcast delay — which is the weakest setting, chosen here deliberately because this preset is the fast one. Fan-out only: least mixing, cheapest, no confirmation wait. Weakest privacy, about two hours.'}};

function applyPreset(p){
 preset=p; const c=PRESETS[p];
 $('#wallets').value=c.wallets; $('#deep').value=c.deep;
 $('#dag_mixing').checked=c.dag_mixing; setFee(c.fee_priority);
 // peel defaults OFF unless the preset asks for it, so switching away from the
 // peel preset clears it rather than silently leaving the slow mode on.
 $('#peel').checked=!!c.peel;
 // THE DELAY IS PART OF THE PRESET. Left out, "Maximum safe" ran at the
 // dropdown's own "WEAKEST" option. Assigned unconditionally (never `if(c...)`)
 // so switching AWAY from a slow preset relaxes it again, the same rule the
 // peel checkbox above already follows.
 $('#hop_delay').value=c.hop_delay||'';
 // Every preset leaves clearnet relay OFF — a preset should never quietly
 // widen the IP exposure, and turning it on is a deliberate per-run override.
 if('clearnet' in c) $('#allow_clearnet_relay').checked=c.clearnet;
 $('#preset-note').textContent=c.note;
 $$('#presets button').forEach(b=>b.setAttribute('aria-pressed',String(b.dataset.p===p)));
 // A preset sets wallets/deep PROGRAMMATICALLY, which fires no 'input' event,
 // so the fee total would keep showing the previous preset's figure -- and the
 // presets are exactly where wallets/deep swing hardest (8/1 to 20/6).
 if(typeof scheduleFees==='function') scheduleFees();
 sync();
}
$$('#presets button').forEach(b=>b.addEventListener('click',()=>applyPreset(b.dataset.p)));

let feePriority=2, feeData=null;
function renderFees(){
 const names=['1 · default','2 · elevated','3 · priority','4 · flash'];
 // feeData.xmr is null whenever the daemon could not supply an estimate --
 // the console shows no number rather than a made-up one, so this must not
 // assume an array. Indexing it blindly used to throw and kill the panel.
 const vals=(feeData && Array.isArray(feeData.xmr))?feeData.xmr:null;
 const imp=!!(feeData&&feeData.implausible);
 // TOTAL for the run, not one transaction. The panel used to show only the
 // per-tx fee while the operator was choosing --wallets and --deep, the two
 // settings that decide how MANY transactions there are, so the number never
 // moved when they changed it. total_xmr is what the run actually reserves.
 const tot=(feeData && Array.isArray(feeData.total_xmr))?feeData.total_xmr:null;
 $('#fees').innerHTML=names.map((n,i)=>`<button class="fee${imp?' imp':''}" data-p="${i+1}" aria-pressed="${feePriority===i+1}">
   <div class="p">${esc(n)}</div><div class="v">${tot?esc(tot[i])+' XMR':(vals?esc(vals[i])+' XMR':'<span class="noest">no estimate</span>')}</div>${tot&&vals?`<div class="pertx">${esc(vals[i])} × ${esc(feeData.rounds)} rounds</div>`:''}</button>`).join('');
 $$('.fee').forEach(b=>b.addEventListener('click',()=>{setFee(+b.dataset.p);sync();}));
 if(!feeData){$('#fsrc').textContent='loading…';return;}
 // The chain first: a fee is meaningless if it is not the chain you think.
 const ntw=feeData&&feeData.nettype_warning;
 $('#fsrc').innerHTML=(ntw?`<div class="warnbox" style="margin-bottom:7px">${esc(ntw)}</div>`:'')
   +(tot?`TOTAL reserved across ${esc(feeData.rounds)} rounds at ${esc($('#wallets').value)} wallets · depth ${esc($('#deep').value)} · `:(vals?'estimate per ~2 kB tx · ':''))+esc(feeData.source)
   +(feeData.warning?`<div class="warnbox" style="margin-top:7px">${esc(feeData.warning)}</div>`:'')
   +(vals?'':`<div style="margin-top:7px"><button id="dfind" class="pfre" type="button">Find my daemon</button></div>`);
 const df=$('#dfind'); if(df) df.addEventListener('click',detectDaemon);
}
// "No estimate" almost always means the daemon field points somewhere nothing
// is listening. Probe the usual loopback ports instead of making the operator
// guess which one their node uses.
async function detectDaemon(){
 const b=$('#dfind'); if(b){b.disabled=true;b.textContent='looking…';}
 let r=null;
 try{ r=await fetch('/api/detect-daemon',{method:'POST',headers:HDR,
   body:JSON.stringify({params:collect()})}).then(x=>x.json()); }
 catch(e){ r={ok:false,detail:'lookup failed: '+e}; }
